/** 

@page benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole_generic_settings Generic settings

This page describes some generic settings, how to set up the environment for 
the Single Black Hole runs, and also how to do some fundamental postprocessing.

# Prepare Peano, ExaHyPE and ExaGRyPE

<div style="background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  This benchmark requires a third-party tool: You will need the GNU Scientific Library (GSL) installed on your system. 
</div>


For this single black hole benchmark, the following Peano toolboxes are mandatory:
  
  - blockstructured (```--enable-blockstructured```)
  - exahype2 (```--enable-exahype2```)
  
In theory, the benchmark passes without parallelisation. In practice, it is so 
computationally demanding that we recommend to use multithreading and MPI. Due 
to this, you also need

   - load balancing (```--enable-load-balancing```)

GPU support is, as always, optional. Other advanced features such as particle 
tracers are not used. 


# Generic build instructions

Builds always resemble:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python:../../../../applications/exahype2/ccz4:../../../../src

python3 specific-python-script
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For each script, the parameter ```--help``` makes the Python script yield further info.

Depending on your system, you might have to load the GSL module separately
before you build (and run) the application:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load gsl
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~




# Clean up old and temporary files

The following statements remove all the glue code and autogenerated C++ files.
There is no risk in deleting them - if you rerun the Python script, they will 
all be reinstantiated.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *.o *.cmake
rm README-*.md
rm Makefile CMakeLists.txt
rm Abstract*
rm -rf celldata facedata globaldata observers tasks vertexdata repositories
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To get rid of the output files, type in

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *patch-file *vtu *.pvd *.bak *.csv
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Visualise outcome

If you have set up your experiment via the Python script with the -plot 
command, you can use the standard Peano scripts to convert 
@ref documentation_tarch_visualisation "Peano's patch files":


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python
/opt/paraview/bin/pvpython ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py --filter-fine-grid solution-CCZ4SBH_FV.peano-patch-file
/opt/paraview/bin/pvpython ~/git/Peano/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py --filter-fine-grid solution-CCZ4SBH_FD4.peano-patch-file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The picture below is taken from @ref tutorials_exahype2_44_localisation "ExaHyPE's coupling discussion" and
illustrates that the higher order solver covers the whole domain, while the FV 
is only active in a tiny cross-shaped region within.

@image html benchmarks/exahype2/ccz4/single-black-hole/grid-setup.png


*/
